WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.780
Fast warp-specialized kernels split each thread block

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.860
into roles that run at the same time —

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.160
one loading, one multiplying, one writing results —

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.260
and hand off work through asynchronous copies and

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.280
barriers that count bytes.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.940
Such a kernel is a concurrent program,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.340
and it fails like one.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.680
A missing wait does not crash anything:

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.620
in some schedules, the output is silently wrong;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.480
in others, the same kernel computes the right result.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.740
A test sees only the schedules that happen to occur.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.860
SentryWard is a verifier for these kernels.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.480
Given a kernel's PTX,

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.540
a launch configuration and a PyTorch reference,

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.640
it proves that the kernel's barriers behave the same way

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.500
in every schedule, that no schedule has a data race,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.800
and that the kernel computes the same function as the

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:45.400
reference.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.870
The same analysis rules out deadlocks and uses of freed

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:49.830
resources.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.810
When a check fails, SentryWard reports a witness.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:53.670
Six parts, one chain:

00:01:07.044 --> 00:01:09.484
Part one specializes the kernel for its launch:

00:01:09.744 --> 00:01:12.144
branches resolved, loops unrolled,

00:01:12.524 --> 00:01:14.484
threads that behave alike grouped together.

00:01:15.044 --> 00:01:16.564
Part two records the program twice:

00:01:16.804 --> 00:01:19.604
as events, for when things happen, and as values,

00:01:19.804 --> 00:01:20.624
for what they compute.

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:23.954
Part three checks that every barrier behaves the same way

00:01:23.954 --> 00:01:25.194
in every schedule,

00:01:25.194 --> 00:01:27.614
and turns the synchronization into a happens-before graph.

00:01:28.414 --> 00:01:31.094
Part four requires every pair of conflicting accesses to

00:01:31.094 --> 00:01:32.354
be ordered by that graph.

00:01:32.534 --> 00:01:36.074
A missing order is a race, reported with its two accesses.

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:38.579
Part five follows one serialization,

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:41.319
which stands for all of them, and eliminates memory:

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:43.979
each output becomes an expression of the inputs.

00:01:44.788 --> 00:01:47.368
Part six checks that expression against the PyTorch

00:01:47.368 --> 00:01:48.428
reference, part by part.

00:01:49.838 --> 00:01:52.078
Of 169 kernels in the benchmark,

00:01:52.538 --> 00:01:56.398
SentryWard verified 52 and found problems in 28.

00:01:56.758 --> 00:01:59.978
The rest it reports as incomplete, never as a pass.

00:02:00.348 --> 00:02:01.948
It found bugs in popular libraries:

00:02:02.148 --> 00:02:04.888
a buffer TileLang reuses while the tensor core is still

00:02:04.888 --> 00:02:05.488
reading it,

00:02:05.448 --> 00:02:08.868
a signal CUTLASS sends before the write it announces has

00:02:08.868 --> 00:02:11.388
landed, and a missing barrier in PyTorch,

00:02:11.488 --> 00:02:12.488
already fixed upstream.

00:02:13.348 --> 00:02:14.808
Because SentryWard checks every schedule,

00:02:14.808 --> 00:02:18.148
it can also show that a synchronization operation is

00:02:18.148 --> 00:02:18.748
unnecessary:

00:02:19.508 --> 00:02:21.768
if the kernel still verifies without it,

00:02:22.048 --> 00:02:23.348
the operation is redundant.

00:02:24.048 --> 00:02:26.368
For kernel writers, evidence for every failed check.

00:02:26.528 --> 00:02:29.488
For kernel agents, a result a machine can act on.

00:02:29.708 --> 00:02:32.668
For library maintainers, synchronization that can go.

00:02:33.448 --> 00:02:34.048
SentryWard.

00:02:33.948 --> 00:02:37.568
Formal verification of warp-specialized GPU kernels.

